WEBVTT

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:22.800
<v Ira Glass>So a little while back, this guy named Ken Irwin started reaching out to people here at This American Life. Emanuele got a voicemail that was both very insistent and deeply apologetic.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，一个名叫肯·欧文（Ken Irwin）的人开始联系我们《美国生活》（This American Life）的同事。伊曼纽尔（Emanuele）收到了一条语音留言，对方既显得非常急切，又语气诚恳地连连道歉。

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:31.220
<v Ken Erwin>Hi, I'm so sorry for calling you. I'm just trying to get ahold of someone at This American Life or New York Times. I'm so sorry for interrupting you on your weekend.
<v Ken Erwin>嗨，很抱歉打扰你。我只是想联系一下《美国生活》或者《纽约时报》的人。很抱歉在周末打扰你，真的非常抱歉。

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:38.810
<v Ira Glass>Chloee got messages on LinkedIn saying, quote, "If you can give me five minutes to tell you what I know so far, I promise it's a crazy story."
<v Ira Glass>克洛伊（Chloee）在领英（LinkedIn）上也收到了他的信息，上面写着：“如果你能给我五分钟告诉我你目前所知道的情况，我保证这是一个疯狂的故事。”

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:49.880
<v Ken Erwin>And today is about to be a million times crazier. Two weeks ago, my life was normal. I literally was just working for Amazon. I was a normal person.
<v Ken Erwin>而今天，这个故事将变得更加疯狂千万倍。两周前，我的生活还很正常。我当时就在亚马逊工作。我只是个普通人。

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:57.090
<v Ira Glass>On Dana's voicemail, he said he was sure she was way too busy to call back herself. But he swore a factchecker could confirm everything he's saying.
<v Ira Glass>在达纳（Dana）的语音留言里，他说他肯定她太忙了，没法亲自回电。但他发誓，会有事实核查员能证实他所说的一切。

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:05.600
<v Ken Erwin>Basically, I became a multimillionaire over the last week and a half, which is insane. And the story is so much crazier than what I'm even saying.
<v Ken Erwin>基本上，我在过去的一周半里变成了一个千万富翁，这太疯狂了。而且这个故事比我刚才说的还要疯狂得多。

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:19.750
<v Ira Glass>To Brian, this guy wrote on Twitter, "I have no idea where the story will go as I'm stuck in it trying to figure it out. But I think you're exactly the type of person that could figure it out. So they all passed that information to me.
<v Ira Glass>至于布莱恩（Brian），这家伙在推特上写道：“我不知道这个故事会走向何方，因为我正深陷其中，试图理清头绪。但我认为你正是那种能把它搞明白的人。”于是他们都把这些信息转给了我。

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.040
<v Ira Glass>[RINGTONE]
<v Ira Glass>[铃声]

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.240
<v Ira Glass>Then I called the guy.
<v Ira Glass>然后我给那个人打了电话。

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.380
<v Ken Erwin>Hi, this is Ken.
<v Ken Erwin>嗨，我是肯。

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.650
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Ken. This is Ira Glass from This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，肯。我是《美国生活》的艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.190
<v Ken Erwin>Oh my god. That is amazing. Hi.
<v Ken Erwin>天哪。太不可思议了。你好。

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:31.520
<v Ira Glass>Hi.
<v Ira Glass>你好。

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.220
<v Ken Erwin>Nice to meet you.
<v Ken Erwin>很高兴见到你。

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.930
<v Ira Glass>Nice to meet you.
<v Ira Glass>很高兴见到你。

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:40.970
<v Ken Erwin>Thank you for calling. I did not expect to be talking to you. I'm so sorry for spamming your entire company.
<v Ken Erwin>谢谢你回电。我没想到能和你通话。对于给你们整个公司发垃圾信息，我深表歉意。

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:54.020
<v Ira Glass>Right off the bat, he asked me if I knew what NFTs are. And I had a general idea that they're images and other things in the crypto world that people are selling to each other for a lot of money. For now, can I say, that's all you need to know for this to make sense.
<v Ira Glass>一上来，他就问我知不知道什么是NFT。我当时有个大概的了解，知道它们是加密货币世界里的图像或其他东西，人们正以高价互相买卖。暂时来说，我可以这么说，这就是你需要了解的全部，这样下面的内容才讲得通。

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.360
<v Ira Glass>He told me that this all began for him two weeks before our conversation on August 22nd.
<v Ira Glass>他告诉我，这一切对他来说始于我们谈话前两周，即8月22日。

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:12.500
<v Ken Erwin>So the person named Adam McBride reached out. If you Google him, he calls himself an NFT archaeologist. I don't know how to describe him. He has a podcast.
<v Ken Erwin>有一个叫亚当·麦克布莱德（Adam McBride）的人联系了他。如果你在谷歌上搜他，会发现他自称是“NFT考古学家”。我不知道该怎么描述他。他有个播客。

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:19.820
<v Ken Erwin>So he reached on LinkedIn and Gmail and basically told me, hey, if you're the owner of PixelMap, I need to talk to you immediately.
<v Ken Erwin>他在领英和Gmail上联系我，基本上就是跟我说：嘿，如果你是PixelMap的拥有者，我需要立刻和你谈谈。

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.000
<v Ira Glass>PixelMap was a website that Ken created in 2016.
<v Ira Glass>PixelMap是肯在2016年创建的一个网站。

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:36.940
<v Ken Erwin>He wants to tell me that basically, I could make a lot of money if I brought the site back up. And I immediately assumed he was scamming me. Because we all get emails like that all the time.
<v Ken Erwin>他想告诉我，基本上，如果我把网站重新上线，我能赚很多钱。我立刻以为他在诈骗我。因为我们总是收到那样的邮件。

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:49.010
<v Ken Erwin>And the only reason I talked to him was he mentioned PixelMap. The site has been down literally since 2017. So I just wanted to know how he even knew that word.
<v Ken Erwin>我之所以愿意和他谈，唯一的原因是他提到了PixelMap。那个网站实际上从2017年起就瘫痪了。所以我只是想知道他到底是怎么知道那个词的。

00:02:49.010 --> 00:03:03.830
<v Ira Glass>Ken has made PixelMap just for fun and to teach himself a new programming language. He's somebody who was always tinkering, inventing things, a hovercraft that could carry a dog. He has three dachshunds and a fourth dog that he refers to as the randomly generated dog.
<v Ira Glass>肯制作PixelMap只是为了好玩，顺便自学一门新的编程语言。他一直是个喜欢修补、发明东西的人，比如他发明过一艘能载狗的汽垫船。他有三只腊肠犬，还有第四只狗，他称之为“随机生成的狗”。

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:20.630
<v Ira Glass>One of his recent projects, he used a 3D printer to create another, better 3D printer. His real job was he worked for Amazon's web services company. He was one of the lead engineers developing code for big companies like Snapchat and Pinterest. He's 33, lives in Indiana.
<v Ira Glass>他最近的项目之一是用一台3D打印机制造了另一台更好的3D打印机。他的正职是在亚马逊的云服务公司工作。他是开发Snapchat和Pinterest等大公司代码的首席工程师之一。他33岁，住在印第安纳州。

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:22.190
<v Ira Glass>As for PixelMap--
<v Ira Glass>关于PixelMap——

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.120
<v Ken Erwin>You can see it. Actually, you're going to think it looks stupid, probably. But-- [LAUGHS]
<v Ken Erwin>你可以看一看。说实话，你可能会觉得它看起来很蠢。但是—— [笑声]

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:46.400
<v Ira Glass>This is the kind of self-deprecating remark I've learned that Ken does reflexively a lot. But in this case, the PixelMap.io website does look kind of primitive when you first see it, like early 8-bit Nintendo or something. It's a grid of tiles, a picture of 4,000 little tiles, and they're tiny. Each is 16 by 16 pixels.
<v Ira Glass>这就是我所了解到的肯习惯性的自我调侃。但在这种情况下，当你第一次看到PixelMap.io网站时，它看起来确实有点原始，就像早期的8位任天堂游戏一样。它是一个瓷砖网格，由4000个小瓷砖组成的图片，它们非常小。每个瓷砖只有16x16像素。

00:03:46.400 --> 00:04:05.820
<v Ira Glass>And the idea is anybody can buy the tiles, own them, and decorate them how they want. So people have different cartoons in them and words they've posted. When Ken created this back in 2016, barely anybody noticed or cared. Maybe 20 or 30 people bought tiles. Within a year, he was pretty much done with PixelMap.
<v Ira Glass>它的理念是任何人都可以购买瓷砖，拥有它们，并按照自己的意愿进行装饰。所以人们在里面贴了各种卡通画和文字。当肯在2016年创建它时，几乎没人注意到或关心。可能也就20或30个人买了瓷砖。一年之内，他基本上就不再管PixelMap了。

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:15.120
<v Ira Glass>So that is the project that this guy Adam McBride wanted to talk to him about. And so the same day that Ken gets Adam's email, that afternoon, he gets on a Zoom with him.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就是亚当·麦克布莱德想和他谈的那个项目。就在肯收到亚当邮件的当天下午，他就和对方进行了Zoom会议。

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:40.350
<v Ken Erwin>He said basically what he does is he goes in and tries to find really old NFT projects and that he does it as a hobby and really enjoys doing it. And he said that mine would be one of the earliest, even though 2016 is not that long ago. In the NFT space, that's almost the oldest you can go. And that I'm probably sitting on a ton of money and I don't realize it.
<v Ken Erwin>他说，他所做的工作基本上就是去寻找非常古老的NFT项目，他把这当作一种业余爱好，并且非常乐在其中。他说我的项目可能是最早的之一，尽管2016年离现在并不久。但在NFT领域，这几乎是最古老的项目了。他还说我可能正坐拥巨额财富而不自知。

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:59.260
<v Ira Glass>Sitting on a ton of money because in the crypto world, one kind of NFT that people get excited about and pay money for is what they call historic NFTs, the first NFTs that ever existed. Ken tells Adam that's all great, what's an NFT? Adam give him a quick primer.
<v Ira Glass>坐拥巨额财富，因为在加密货币世界里，有一种NFT让人们很兴奋并愿意花钱购买，那就是所谓的“历史性NFT”，即有史以来最早的NFT。肯问亚当：那很好，什么是NFT？亚当给了他一个简短的入门介绍。

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.830
<v Ira Glass>And had it ever occurred to you that what you had made was an NFT?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有想过，你所创造的东西其实是一个NFT？

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:26.660
<v Ken Erwin>No, because when I made it, there were no NFTs. The only time I had heard of NFT since then, I had heard in the news that Steph Curry had one. And I really hadn't even looked to see what an NFT was, which I know sounds really stupid. I had truly completely forgotten about my site.
<v Ken Erwin>没有，因为我创作的时候，根本没有NFT这回事。从那以后我唯一一次听说NFT是在新闻里看到斯蒂芬·库里（Steph Curry）拥有一个。我甚至都没去查过NFT到底是什么，我知道这听起来很蠢。我真的已经完全忘了我的网站了。

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:42.590
<v Ken Erwin>And I was saying, how much do you think I could get for it? And he said, I've seen projects like this. I had one that the developer got over 100,000. But looking at yours, I think you could probably get 500 grand out of it.
<v Ken Erwin>我当时问他，你觉得我能卖多少钱？他说，我见过类似的项目。有一个开发者卖了超过10万。但看着你的项目，我觉得你可能能赚到50万。

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:54.440
<v Ken Erwin>And at that point, I thought he was really nice. I didn't think there was any chance on earth of that actually happening. [LAUGHS]
<v Ken Erwin>在那一刻，我觉得他真的很不错。我根本没想过这事儿真的会发生。 [笑声]

00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:12.380
<v Ira Glass>Adam McBride tells him, I can help you set this up and get it relaunched, and people will pay you gobs of money for the rest of those 4,000 tiles. He said he knows a group of guys who love the early historic NFTs, like this one. And they'll all talk it up on Twitter. They'll encourage people to buy. He says he's done this for a bunch of other old NFTs.
<v Ira Glass>亚当·麦克布莱德告诉他：我可以帮你设置好一切，重新发布它，人们会为你剩下的那4000个瓷砖支付大把钞票。他说他认识一群喜欢早期历史性NFT的人，就像这个项目一样。他们会在推特上大肆宣传。他们会鼓励人们购买。他说他已经为其他很多老式NFT做过这件事了。

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:49.340
<v Ira Glass>But Adam tells him there was one little snag. Ken needs to lower the price of each tile. That's because back in 2016, Ken set the price of each tile at 2 Ether. Now, Ether-- I didn't know anything about this. Ken I to explain this to me-- is the name of one of the big cryptocurrencies, like Bitcoin. And 2 Ether back in 2016 was around 20 bucks. The problem, Adam explained, was the value of Ether had blown up. And 2 Ether these days was now more than $6,000, way too high for most people.
<v Ira Glass>但亚当告诉他有一个小障碍。肯需要降低每个瓷砖的价格。因为早在2016年，肯把每个瓷砖的价格定为2以太币（Ether）。现在，以太币——我对这个一无所知，肯得向我解释——是像比特币一样的主流加密货币之一。2016年的2以太币价值约为20美元。亚当解释说，问题在于以太币的价值已经暴涨了。现在的2以太币价值已经超过6000美元，这对大多数人来说太高了。

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:56.810
<v Ken Erwin>He said I would be a lot better off and a lot more successful if I would set all of them to 0.35 Eth.
<v Ken Erwin>他说如果我把所有瓷砖的价格都设为0.35以太币，我会过得更好，也会更成功。

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:06.080
<v Ira Glass>0.35 Eth. In other words, each image would cost 0.35 Ether, a third of an Ether-- at the time, around $1,000.
<v Ira Glass>0.35以太币。换句话说，每张图像的价格为0.35以太币，也就是三分之一个以太币——当时大约是1000美元。

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.930
<v Ken Erwin>And for me, that made complete sense.
<v Ken Erwin>对我来说，这完全说得通。

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:13.280
<v Ira Glass>He told me he didn't really believe any of this was going to work anyway. But he figured, what's he got to lose?
<v Ira Glass>他告诉我，他其实根本不相信这一切会成功。但他心想，反正也没什么可损失的？

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.560
<v Ken Erwin>So I'm just like, I'll go with it.
<v Ken Erwin>所以我就是想，那就试试吧。

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:32.630
<v Ira Glass>So Ken started knocking down the price of his 4,000 tiles, which actually took some time. Because the way he wrote the original code, the original program for PixelMap, repricing each tile took a few minutes. And had to do them by hand one at a time for hundreds and hundreds of tiles.
<v Ira Glass>于是肯开始降低他的4000个瓷砖的价格，这其实花了不少时间。因为他编写原始代码的方式，即PixelMap的原始程序，修改每个瓷砖的价格需要几分钟。他不得不为成百上千个瓷砖一个个手动操作。

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:47.090
<v Ira Glass>Now, he could have written code to automate that process. But he said he wasn't thinking straight that particular week. Because that week, his dad had a heart attack and went to the hospital for bypass surgery. And so Ken was spending a lot of time at the hospital with his dad.
<v Ira Glass>其实他本可以写一段代码来自动化这个过程。但他那天头脑不太清醒。因为那一周，他父亲心脏病发作并住院进行了搭桥手术。所以肯花了很多时间在医院陪他父亲。

00:07:47.090 --> 00:08:04.670
<v Ira Glass>Adam was sending messages to the buyers, who were, quote, "pounding at the door." Hurry up, do them faster. And some people were so impatient, they just paid the higher price, over $6,000 2 Ether, which leads to an obvious question.
<v Ira Glass>亚当一直在给买家发信息，说他们“正在疯狂抢购”。快点，弄快点。有些买家太没耐心，直接支付了更高的价格，即超过6000美元的2以太币，这就引出了一个显而易见的问题。

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:11.630
<v Ira Glass>Why lower the price if people were paying 2 Ethereum for them?
<v Ira Glass>既然人们愿意支付2以太币，为什么要降价呢？

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:33.870
<v Ken Erwin>Because I'm a moron. And he said that the whole reason that he was doing this big campaign-- I have no idea. My wife-- she's not my wife, but I've been with her for 11 years. She basically was like, if they're buying them at this, shouldn't we raise the price to 0.5 or 1 or just leave it at 2? Like, it looks like they want it.
<v Ken Erwin>因为我就是个白痴。他说他进行这场大营销的全部原因——我不知道。我的妻子——其实她不是我妻子，但我们在一起11年了。她基本上觉得，如果他们愿意按这个价格买，我们不是应该把价格提高到0.5或1，或者干脆保持2不变吗？看起来他们很想要。

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.690
<v Ken Erwin>And I was like, no. It was not smart. It was--
<v Ken Erwin>我却说：不。那不聪明。那简直是——

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:38.000
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:59.270
<v Ken Erwin>I was worried that-- what if they all of a sudden disappear or change their mind? I have no idea like why they want it, what's going on. All I know is if we sell them right now, I had done the math. I was like, we're looking at 2 million or more if we sell all of them.
<v Ken Erwin>我当时担心的是——如果他们突然消失或改变主意了怎么办？我完全不知道为什么他们想要它，发生了什么。我只知道如果现在卖掉，我算过这笔账。我当时想，如果我们全部卖掉，我们将获得200万甚至更多。

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:09.280
<v Ken Erwin>And I've never had that kind of money. Literally, all I could think about was like, I'm about to become a millionaire. And then the weird part of the story started happening.
<v Ken Erwin>我从来没见过那么多钱。我脑子里唯一能想到的就是：我马上要成为百万富翁了。然后这个故事诡异的部分就开始发生了。

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.920
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, the weird part of the story hasn't even happened yet?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，故事诡异的部分还没发生吗？

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:18.770
<v Ken Erwin>Correct, yes.
<v Ken Erwin>没错，是的。

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:35.150
<v Ira Glass>What transpired next, he got a phone call that made him rethink everything that had happened to this point and look very differently at this guy Adam McBride, who seemed so nice and helpful at first. He and I had a series of conversations over the course of weeks where all of this unfolded.
<v Ira Glass>接下来发生的事情是，他接到了一通电话，让他重新审视到目前为止发生的一切，并用完全不同的眼光看待亚当·麦克布莱德这个起初看起来既友善又乐于助人的家伙。在接下来的几周里，我们进行了一系列对话，整个事情才慢慢揭开。

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:59.930
<v Ira Glass>Basically, Ken started off like most of us, I think. He hadn't been following this whole NFT thing, didn't know what they were. And then suddenly, like, I don't know, somebody in a fairy tale who's transported to a strange new world where he learns he's a prince or a knight or something, Ken gets pulled into this crypto world where he finds he has all this cred, all this street cred as one of the first pioneers.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，肯起初就像我们大多数人一样。他没关注过NFT这回事，不知道它们是什么。然后突然间，就像童话里那些被传送到奇异新世界的人，发现自己竟是王子或骑士，肯被拉进了这个加密世界，发现自己作为最早的先驱之一竟拥有如此高的声望。

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:17.420
<v Ira Glass>And once he's there, he starts to take a look around at the NFT world, sort of dazed. Like, OK, you just made me a millionaire. Who are you people? And he does not like what he sees. And then some other things happen after that, which you'll hear.
<v Ira Glass>一旦进入那里，他开始晕乎乎地环顾四周。就像是：好吧，你刚刚让我成了百万富翁。你们到底是什么人？然后他不喜欢他所看到的一切。在那之后还发生了一些别的事情，你会听到的。

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:30.410
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Seriously, stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听，别走开。

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:42.910
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Planet of the Apes. OK, so Ken repriced his tiles, and things went even better than Adam McBride said they might. He sold 3,000 tiles, earning him a total of $3.5 million.
