<v Adam McBride>当我和亚当交谈时，我惊讶地发现亚当是在把肯拉进那个世界前仅仅六个月才沉迷上NFT的。基本情况是去年三月，一批所谓的“历史性、老式NFT被重新发现，并开始赚大钱。”

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:33.360
<v Ira Glass>I was surprised to learn when I talk to Adam that Adam got hooked on NFTs only six months before he pulled Ken into that world. Basically what happened is March of last year, a bunch of these, quote unquote, "historic, old NFTs were rediscovered and started making tons of money."
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是，这直接翻转了我大脑里的开关，哇塞。就像是，天哪，有金矿。去拿你的铲子。去挖。因为那里有金子。你要做的就是去找到它。

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:46.230
<v Adam McBride>I mean, it just flipped the switch in my brain, like, holy cow. It was literally like, oh my god, there's gold. Get your shovel. Go dig. Because there's gold there. All you got to do is go find it.
<v Adam McBride>接下来的四个月，他说他每天花10个小时通过谷歌搜索“数字收藏品”、“区块链艺术”、“区块链收藏品”等词汇来寻找其他早期NFT。他找到了15或20个似乎可能有效的古老NFT。但他表示，它们大多数在原始编程或其他制作方面存在问题，导致它们不适合今天出售。

00:42:46.230 --> 00:43:05.970
<v Ira Glass>The next four months, he says he spent 10 hours a day searching for other early NFTs by googling phrases like digital collectibles, blockchain art, blockchain collectibles. He found 15 or 20 old NFTs that seemed like they might work. But most of them, he says there was some problem in the original programming or whatever they made them unsuitable to sell today.
<v Ira Glass>他确实找到了一些合适的。然后他和朋友发现了PixelMap。从一开始，他们就认为，如果能说服创造者降价，这会是一个巨大的成功。而他们降价那么多的原因？嗯，到那时，亚当已经有了一些经验。

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:22.320
<v Ira Glass>He did find a few that were suitable. And then he and a friend found PixelMap. And from the beginning, they thought it would be a big hit if they could just convince its creator to lower the price. And the reason that they lowered it so much? Well, by that point, Adam had a little experience.
<v Ira Glass>我唯一搞砸的那个NFT重启项目，我们把价格定得太高了，结果失败了。

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:28.410
<v Adam McBride>And the one NFT relaunch I failed at, we set that price too high, and it failed.
<v Adam McBride>是哪一个？

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:29.470
<v Ira Glass>Which one was this?
<v Ira Glass>叫Etherization。

00:43:29.470 --> 00:43:31.170
<v Adam McBride>It's called Etherization.
<v Adam McBride>人们买得不够多。

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:32.430
<v Ira Glass>People didn't buy enough of them.
<v Ira Glass>他们买得不够多。

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:34.110
<v Adam McBride>They didn't buy enough of it.
<v Adam McBride>所以他为PixelMap的价格做出了最好的猜测。结果比他之前或之后做过的任何NFT项目都要好。人们简直疯了。

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:40.840
<v Ira Glass>So he made his best guess for PixelMap's price. And it worked out better than any NFT he's done before or since. People just went nuts.
<v Ira Glass>是的，那是一枚火箭，完全是一枚火箭。

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:43.920
<v Adam McBride>Yeah, it was a rocket ship, a complete rocket ship.
<v Adam McBride>你买了多少瓷砖？

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:45.780
<v Ira Glass>How many tiles did you buy?
<v Ira Glass>三个。 [笑声] 我是个白痴。我是个白痴。这很有趣，因为我当时比起赚钱，更多是在追求名声和认可。我甚至不知道该说什么。是的。这更多像是：看，麦克布莱德。他重新发现了这个东西。这太棒了。

00:43:45.780 --> 00:44:09.120
<v Adam McBride>Three. [LAUGHS] I'm an idiot. I'm an idiot. It's funny because I was more looking for the fame and recognition than the money. I don't even know what to say. Yeah. It was more about like, look at McBride. He rediscovered this. This is awesome.
<v Adam McBride>好了，澄清一下，他确实靠这个赚了五十万美元，也就是肯作为感谢给他的那笔钱。但他当时并不知道会发生那种事。他说，通常情况下，NFT的创造者什么都不会给他。而且没人给过他接近这个数目的钱。

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:22.370
<v Ira Glass>OK, to be clear, he did make a half million dollars off this, the money that Ken handed him as a thank you. But he didn't know that was going to happen. Usually, he says, the NFT creators give him nothing. And nobody's given him close to this much.
<v Ira Glass>他确实想在NFT领域赚钱。但亚当·麦克布莱德似乎同样想要的是，他想让历史性NFT成为一种流行趋势，让它们获得尊重，并建立一个更大的社区来欣赏它们。这就是他的切入点。

00:44:22.370 --> 00:44:39.080
<v Ira Glass>He does want to make money in NFTs. But what Adam McBride also wants, just as much, it seems, is that he wants to make historic NFTs a thing, get them respect, create a much larger community that's into them. That's his angle.
<v Ira Glass>如果他成功了，是的，他会从他个人持有的所有历史性NFT中获利。但这现在也成了他的一种个人使命。他希望历史性NFT被铭记并被重视。

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:50.150
<v Ira Glass>If he succeeds, yeah, he'll make money on all the historical NFTs that he owns personally. But it's also now become kind of a personal mission for him. He wants historic NFTs to be remembered and valued.
<v Ira Glass>正是出于这个原因，他并没有用自己的NFT进行“拉高出货”。没有出货。他仍然持有PixelMap的瓷砖以及他发行的其他历史性NFT。肯确实用Chainalysis工具查看了亚当的交易，证实在他能找到的所有交易中，亚当为他的每个项目都买了几个NFT，然后基本上就一直持有它们。

00:44:50.150 --> 00:45:15.480
<v Ira Glass>And for that reason, he's not pumping and dumping with his own NFTs. There's no dumping. He's still holding on to his tiles of PixelMap and the other historic NFTs he's launched. Ken actually looked at Adams transactions with the Chainalysis tool and confirmed that in all the transactions that he could find, Adam buys a few NFTs of each of his projects and then basically just holds onto them.
<v Ira Glass>至于“拉高出货”计划的另一个大特点，即欺骗，我找不到任何亚当撒谎炒作他推广的NFT或虚假承诺人们能赚多少钱的例子。当谈到我采访的律师们关于亚当以特别低价向50个“猿群”成员发布NFT Aether的担忧时——提醒你一下，他们说，基本上，如果人们能得到更低的价格，亚当需要向全世界、向任何可能考虑购买Aether的人披露这一点。

00:45:15.480 --> 00:45:48.320
<v Ira Glass>As for the other big feature of a pump and dump scheme, deception, I couldn't find any examples of Adam lying about the NFTs he promotes or making false promises about how much money people would make. When it comes to the other concerns of the lawyers that I talked to about Adam and the way that he released that NFT Aether to 50 of his apes at a special low price-- just to remind you, they said, basically, if people were getting a lower price, Adam needed to disclose that to the world, to anybody who might be considering buying Aether.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我确实这么做了。像Aether，在我们要进行任何赠品或机会之前，它就已经在推特上了。任何人都可以加入那个Discord。任何人都可以介入。我们需要更多人加入。

00:45:48.320 --> 00:46:02.480
<v Adam McBride>Well, I actually do do that. Like with Aether, it was on Twitter before we did any sort of giveaway or opportunity. Anybody could have joined that Discord. Anybody could have gotten in. We wanted more people in.
<v Adam McBride>那根本不是事实。交谈结束后，我回去查看了亚当当时发过的推特。他从没提到过你可以通过参加这个活动获得Aether的极低价格，也没提到他举办过这个活动。

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:16.910
<v Ira Glass>That's not true at all. After we talked, I went back and looked at Adam's tweets from back then. And he never tweeted about the fact that you could get a really low price on Aether by coming to this event, or that he had held the event at all.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，在Discord上，他对他关注者说的话是：“不要发推特或转发给任何人，我想把这个机会留在我们这个社区内部。保密。”他确实在活动发生后发在Medium的一篇文章中提到了这次赠送活动。所以我们又联系上了。

00:46:16.910 --> 00:46:34.690
<v Ira Glass>In fact, on Discord, he is saying to his followers things like, "Do not tweet or send this out, I want to keep this opportunity to this community only. Keep it on the down low." He did mention the giveaway in a post that he did on Medium after it happened. So we got back on the line.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，艾拉，你好吗？

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:36.340
<v Adam McBride>Hey, Ira, how you doing?
<v Adam McBride>我很好。你呢？

00:46:36.340 --> 00:46:38.530
<v Ira Glass>I'm doing good. How are you doing?
<v Ira Glass>挺好的。记忆真是个有趣的东西，不是吗？ [笑声]

00:46:38.530 --> 00:46:43.520
<v Adam McBride>Pretty good. Memory is a funny thing, isn't it? [LAUGHS]
<v Adam McBride>确实是。

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:44.490
<v Ira Glass>Indeed it is.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我们的大脑编织的网，伙计，太有趣了。

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:48.490
<v Adam McBride>Oh, the webs we weave with our brain, man, it is funny.
<v Adam McBride>他告诉我，当他回头看Aether发生的事情时，他意识到那时他看到了一个利用打折的NFT来奖励他Discord群成员的机会，他称之为“Alpha Discord”。

00:46:48.490 --> 00:47:00.040
<v Ira Glass>He told me that when he went back and looked at what happened with Aether, he realized back then, he saw a chance to use the discounted NFTs to reward the people in his Discord, which he calls the Alpha Discord.
<v Ira Glass>尽管这是一个公共社区，任何人都可以加入。但当时人们在想：如果我们得不到任何“Alpha”信息，我们为什么要在Alpha Discord里？

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:08.950
<v Adam McBride>Even though it's a public community, anybody can join it. At that point in time, people were like, why are we in the Alpha Discord if we're not getting any alpha?
<v Adam McBride>在NFT世界里，“Alpha”意味着特殊信息、情报、别人还没有得到的东西。

00:47:08.950 --> 00:47:14.380
<v Ira Glass>Alpha in the NFT world means special information, intel, stuff everybody else doesn't have yet.
<v Ira Glass>对吧？如果你不提供一些很棒的信息，我们为什么要在这儿？所以我试图奖励那些待了很久的人。

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:23.710
<v Adam McBride>Right? Why are we here if you're not going to give us some awesome info? And so I was trying to reward people who were in there for a while.
<v Adam McBride>他告诉我，这一切都是为了建立一个更大的、重视这些历史性NFT的社区。总的来说，我必须说，亚当没考虑过要披露他为“猿群”成员做的这次特别赠送活动，这恰恰表明他以及整个NFT世界的很多人只是在边走边摸索。每个人都还在摸索哪些法律将适用于他们。

00:47:23.710 --> 00:47:44.320
<v Ira Glass>He told me that this was all in the spirit of creating a larger community of people who value these historic NFTs. In general, I have to say, the fact that Adam did not think to disclose this special giveaway that he did for the apes just shows how much he and so much of the NFT world is just making it up as they go along. Everyone is still figuring out what laws are going to apply to them.
<v Ira Glass>比如，当我和亚当交谈时，有一个时刻，我准确地告诉了他律师认为他应该披露多少信息才聪明，以避免诉讼。这对亚当来说是个新消息。

00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:55.220
<v Ira Glass>Like, there was this moment when I was talking to Adam where I told him exactly how much disclosure the lawyers thought would be smart for him to do to avoid lawsuits. And it was news to Adam.
<v Ira Glass>不，这建议很好。我听进去了。我想我的行为是恰当的。我认为意图很重要。所以我对自己所做的事感觉相当良好。

00:47:55.220 --> 00:48:08.950
<v Adam McBride>No, it's good advice. And I hear it. I think I've acted appropriately. I think intention matters. So I feel pretty good about what I've done.
<v Adam McBride>好吧，那是所有罪犯都会说的话。

00:48:08.950 --> 00:48:10.780
<v Ira Glass>Well, that's what all the criminals say.
<v Ira Glass>[笑声] 嗯，走着瞧吧。艾拉，来监狱看我。请你一定要来监狱看我，好吗？

00:48:10.780 --> 00:48:27.160
<v Adam McBride>[LAUGHS] Well, we'll see. Come visit me in jail, Ira. Will you please come visit me in jail?
<v Adam McBride>距离我最初给肯打电话已经过去半年了，这半年他一直在他被传送到的那个NFT世界里探索。他的行为越来越像个当地人，虽然还算不上“猿群”，但绝对更“能容忍”这些猿群了。

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:42.010
<v Ira Glass>It's now been a half year since my initial phone calls with Ken, half a year where he's been traipsing around the NFT world that he found himself transported to. And he's acting more and more like a local, not exactly an ape yet, but definitely more ape-tolerant.
<v Ira Glass>NFT在他看来似乎不再那么邪恶了。事实上，他曾经认为最糟糕的事情——那些被炒作的NFT，大家在价格上涨时一哄而上，然后卖出获利，留下一群亏损者——他现在自己也参与其中了，在某些项目上赚了钱，在另一些项目上亏了钱。

00:48:42.010 --> 00:49:01.330
<v Ira Glass>NFTs don't seem so evil to him anymore. In fact, the exact thing that he thought was so terrible-- NFTs that get hyped, everybody piles on as the price rises, and then people sell and take a quick profit, leaving lots of losers in their wake-- he's now speculated like that himself, made money on a few things, lost money on others.
<v Ira Glass>他说，对于那些NFT，他认为大多数NFT其实大家心里都清楚价格会涨一阵子然后崩盘。

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:09.070
<v Ira Glass>He says with those NFTs, with most NFTs, he thinks, actually, everybody knows the price is going to go up for a little while and then crash.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都知道这一点，每个人只是都在试图把握时机。

00:49:09.070 --> 00:49:12.220
<v Ken Erwin>And everyone knows that, and everyone's just trying to time it.
<v Ken Erwin>把握卖出的时机。

00:49:12.220 --> 00:49:13.540
<v Ira Glass>Time when they'll sell.
<v Ira Glass>如果你把握得好，那你就能做得不错。这确实感觉像个游戏，某种程度上。我在这个领域待得越久，就越觉得我们大家都在赌场。而且因为没有任何规则，所以才公平。每个人都在玩这种套路。我不认为这有什么道德错误。

00:49:13.540 --> 00:49:37.950
<v Ken Erwin>And if you time it right, then you do pretty well. And it definitely feels like a game, sort of. The more I am in the space, the more it feels like we're all kind of at a casino. And because there aren't any rules, it's fair. Everyone is doing this type of thing. And I don't think there's anything morally wrong with it.
<v Ken Erwin>天哪，听听你说的话。

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:38.770
<v Ira Glass>Oh my god, listen to you.
<v Ira Glass>我不认为——

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:39.130
<v Ken Erwin>I don't think--
<v Ken Erwin>你和你之前简直判若两人。

00:49:39.130 --> 00:49:41.100
<v Ira Glass>You are so different than you were.
<v Ira Glass>[笑声] 也许我已经变坏了。

00:49:41.100 --> 00:49:44.280
<v Ken Erwin>[LAUGHS] Maybe I've been corrupted.
<v Ken Erwin>是什么彻底改变了他？整个NFT世界在他意识到自己没被诈骗的那一刻起就开始变得不同了。突然间，他觉得自己并没有降落在一个满是小偷、试图占他便宜的黑暗森林里。

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:56.130
<v Ira Glass>What changed him, exactly? The whole NFT world started to look different to him the moment he realized he wasn't being scammed. Suddenly it didn't seem like he'd landed in a dark woods full of thieves trying to take advantage of him.
<v Ira Glass>是的，在那之前我之所以那么生气，是因为我以为这一切都是内部勾结，亚当一直在骗我。然后发现他实际上把重启这件事做得非常好，他确实是在帮我——

00:49:56.130 --> 00:50:13.470
<v Ken Erwin>Yeah, the reason why I was so angry until that point was I thought that it was an in-house, that I'd been lied to by Adam this entire time. And then to find out that he actually did a really good job of relaunching it and he actually was helping me--
<v Ken Erwin>对。所以你意识到，噢，等一下。噢，不，这个陌生人实际上为我做了件好事。

00:50:13.470 --> 00:50:18.720
<v Ira Glass>Right. So you realized, oh, wait a second. Oh no, this stranger actually did something good for me.
<v Ira Glass>是的，而且他甚至没收我钱。是的，我给了他小费。但我知道如果他不存在，我也没法自己想出该如何重启并挂牌PixelMap，我肯定做不到这么好。我不知道——那种事情不是我的专业领域。是的，不，他是认真的。

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:41.930
<v Ken Erwin>Yes, and he literally didn't charge me. Like, yes, I tipped him. But I know if he never existed and I somehow knew that I should relaunch PixelMap and list it, I would not have done as good of a job. I had no idea-- anything like that is not my skill set. Yeah, no, he was legit.
<v Ken Erwin>肯还遇到了一群像他一样的开发者，他们不是诈骗犯。他们只是为了好玩才买NFT。就好像他找到了同类，整个NFT世界看起来就没那么阴森了。

00:50:41.930 --> 00:50:52.580
<v Ira Glass>Ken also met a bunch of developers like him, who weren't scammers. They were just buying NFTs for fun. It was like he found his people, and the whole NFT world seemed less ominous.
<v Ira Glass>这并不是说他看得起大多数NFT，他认为99%的NFT基本上就是垃圾，要么是为了捞快钱而故意设计的垃圾，要么本身就是构思错误的产物。其实，这并非非同寻常的观点。有趣的是，我为这个故事采访的那些最大的NFT爱好者，那些真正热爱NFT的人，他们基本都同意这一点。

00:50:52.580 --> 00:51:14.630
<v Ira Glass>Not that he thinks very much of most NFTs, 99% of them, he thinks, are pretty much junk, either intended as junk to make a quick buck or just misconceived ideas in the first place. Actually, this is not an unusual opinion. It was interesting to me that the biggest NFT enthusiasts that I talked to for this story, people who really love NFTs, they pretty much agreed with that.
<v Ira Glass>亚当·麦克布莱德说，今天发布的NFT中99%最终可能都会一文不值。他说大多数人现在可能不应该进入NFT领域。机器人兄弟们给出的数字是90%一文不值。我读到NFT大佬加里·维纳查克（Gary Vaynerchuk）预测98%的NFT会亏钱。

00:51:14.630 --> 00:51:40.910
<v Ira Glass>Adam McBride says 99% of all NFTs released today will probably end up being worth nothing. He says that most people probably should not get into NFTs right now. The Bot Guys put the number at 90% worthless. I read that one of the big NFT moguls, Gary Vaynerchuk, predicted that 98% of NFTs would lose money.
<v Ira Glass>肯现在的日子过得很平静，他依然住在原来的那栋房子里，这是一栋有蓝绿色百叶窗、到处都是地精、小玩意和腊肠犬的维多利亚风格建筑。他用PixelMap的钱还清了抵押贷款，给自己买了一台更好的电脑和一辆跑车（他现在觉得他打算把跑车卖了），缴纳了超过一百万美元的税款。

00:51:40.910 --> 00:52:00.020
<v Ira Glass>Ken's life these days, he's still in the same house he was before all this, a Victorian with turquoise shutters and gnomes and trinkets and dachshunds everywhere. He's paid off the mortgage with his PixelMap money, bought himself a nicer computer and a fancy sports car that now he thinks he's going to get rid of, paid over a million dollars in taxes.
<v Ira Glass>我问过：你的大部分钱还在以太币里吗？它的价格忽高忽低。他解释说，不，为了安全起见，他已经把一半以上的钱转换成了正规的美元。但他表示，拥有那么多钱并没有真的让他的日常生活发生多大变化。他说他工作得比以往任何时候都多，只不过现在全是关于PixelMap的。

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:19.750
<v Ira Glass>I was like, is most of your money still in Ether, whose price goes up and down? And he explained that, no, he's converted more than half of it into regular US dollars, for safety's sake. But he said that having all that money hasn't really changed much in a day-to-day way. He's working more than ever, he said, except now it's all on PixelMap.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得你处于一种奇怪的境地，你工作生涯的大部分时间都在实际操作，为亚马逊、Snapchat这样的大公司编写代码，人们会使用这些代码，它们在世界各地发挥作用。然后是你构建的这个完全没用的东西——

00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:38.020
<v Ira Glass>I feel like you're in this weird situation where most of your working life, you were actually doing things and developing code for big companies, Amazon and Snapchat, and people would use the code, like, it would do stuff all over the world. And then this thing that you build that's completely useless--
<v Ira Glass>我讨厌你。我开玩笑呢。

00:52:38.020 --> 00:52:40.090
<v Ken Erwin>I hate you. I'm just playing
<v Ken Erwin>——对社会没做出什么贡献——

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:41.830
<v Ira Glass>--that doesn't contribute to society really--
<v Ira Glass>不，你没意识到的是，我整个职业生涯都在为无意义的事业做贡献。比如在Salesforce工作时，就是为了确保在黑色星期五那天，你能收到来自百思买（Best Buy）和其他每家公司的垃圾邮件。那就像——

00:52:41.830 --> 00:52:58.450
<v Ken Erwin>No, what you don't realize is I have been contributing to pointless causes my entire career. So when I worked at Salesforce, it was to help make sure that on Black Friday, you get spam emails from like, Best Buy and every other company. And that's like--
<v Ken Erwin>所以你的意思是，你之前是富有成效、对社会有贡献的成员这种想法很荒谬？

00:52:58.450 --> 00:53:03.670
<v Ira Glass>So are you saying this idea of before you were a productive, contributing member of society is ridiculousness?
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是我从来都不是一个有成效的成员。

00:53:03.670 --> 00:53:07.030
<v Ken Erwin>I'm saying I was never a productive member.
<v Ken Erwin>但你有没有想过，让你致富的这个东西——

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:13.630
<v Ira Glass>But do you think about the fact that the thing that made you rich is this thing that doesn't--
<v Ira Glass>真的很奇怪，我可能写了大概一百万行代码——我不知道具体多少行，但很多，而PixelMap只有70行。所以写了70行好玩的东西就能这样，确实很奇怪。

00:53:13.630 --> 00:53:31.000
<v Ken Erwin>It's very odd that I've written probably like a million-- I don't know how many lines, but a lot of lines of code, and PixelMap is 70 lines. So it is very odd that 70 lines that I wrote for fun-- yes, it's very odd.
<v Ken Erwin>我想我能描述出的最好方式是，感觉就像在游戏里找到了作弊码，但在现实生活中生效了。就像，你输入了指令，然后突然间，你的银行账户不再是个问题。这非常奇怪。

00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:50.610
<v Ken Erwin>I think the best way I can describe it is it feels like I found a cheat code, like in a game, but for real life. Like, you type in the thing and then all of a sudden, your bank account isn't an issue. It's very odd.
<v Ken Erwin>他每天花在PixelMap上的时间，有几个小时是在Discord上和拥有瓷砖的人聊天。但占用他大部分时间的是，肯正在为PixelMap网站设计一款视频游戏。他在雇佣音乐家、艺术家、游戏开发者和项目经理，同时也自己写很多代码。他一直想做一款视频游戏。

00:53:50.610 --> 00:54:09.870
<v Ira Glass>The time he spends working on PixelMap every day, a couple of hours of that is chatting on Discord with the people who own tiles. But what's sucking up most of his time, Ken's designing a video game for the PixelMap site. He's paying a musician and an artist and a game developer and a project manager and doing lots of coding himself. He's always wanted to make a video game.
<v Ira Glass>现在他有奢侈的时间随心所欲地做这件事。他知道这款视频游戏不会给他赚任何钱。但他只是喜欢制造东西。正是这种冲动让他最初创建了PixelMap。

00:54:09.870 --> 00:54:35.230
<v Ira Glass>And now he has the luxury of being able to spend as much time as he wants doing that. He knows this video game is not going to make him any money. But he just likes making stuff. That's the impulse that led him to create PixelMap in the first place.
